---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "LMIA Processing Times November 2026: High-Wage LMIAs Now Average 102 Business Days";
const description = "ESDC's November 12, 2026 update: high-wage LMIAs average 102 business days, PR stream 94, low-wage 86. What the wait means for B.C. job offers and work permits.";
const canonical = "https://bcpnpcalculator.ca/articles/lmia-processing-times-november-2026";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How long does an LMIA take in November 2026?",
    a: "ESDC's update published November 12, 2026 gives these averages for October 2026: 9 business days for the Global Talent Stream, 11 for the Seasonal Agricultural Worker Program, 20 for the Agricultural stream, 86 for the low-wage stream, 94 for the permanent resident stream and 102 for the high-wage stream. They are averages, not guarantees, and they do not include the recruitment period before the employer applies.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need an LMIA for the BC PNP?",
    a: "No. WelcomeBC says employers supporting a BC PNP Skills Immigration applicant do not need a federal LMIA. You still need an eligible job offer from a B.C. employer, and you need valid status to keep working in Canada while you wait. After nomination, the BC PNP may issue a work permit support letter that lets you apply for an employer-specific permit without an LMIA, if you meet its conditions.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does an LMIA still add Express Entry points?",
    a: "No. Since November 3, 2026, IRCC says a job offer does not need an LMIA to qualify in Express Entry, and having one does not add Comprehensive Ranking System points or give a profile priority. Points for a job offer now depend on whether it is in an eligible high-wage occupation.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can a Vancouver employer get a low-wage LMIA right now?",
    a: "Generally not. For applications submitted from October 9, 2026 to January 7, 2027, ESDC lists Vancouver's unemployment rate at 7.0%, above the 6% line at which it refuses to process low-wage LMIAs. Exemptions apply, including construction, food manufacturing, hospitals, nursing and residential care facilities, primary agriculture and, since November 6, 2026, early childhood educators.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the high-wage threshold for an LMIA in B.C.?",
    a: "ESDC lists $38.40 an hour for B.C. LMIA applications received on or after July 17, 2026. A job paying at or above that wage goes through the high-wage stream, which currently has the longest average processing time at 102 business days.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a },
  })),
};
---

<ArticleLayout slug="lmia-processing-times-november-2026" datePublished="2026-11-15" dateModified="2026-11-15" articleSection="News" keywords="LMIA processing times, LMIA processing time November 2026, how long does LMIA take, high-wage LMIA processing time, PR stream LMIA processing time, low-wage LMIA B.C., Global Talent Stream processing time, ESDC LMIA wait times" title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} ogImage="/images/articles/lmia-processing-times-november-2026.png">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="inline-block bg-red-100 text-red-800 px-4 py-1 rounded-full text-sm font-bold mb-4 border border-red-200 shadow-sm">
        Immigration News
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        LMIA Processing Times November 2026: High-Wage LMIAs Now Average 102 Business Days
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        Employment and Social Development Canada published its monthly LMIA processing times on November 12, 2026. Here is what the new numbers mean for B.C. employers, workers waiting on a job-offer-based work permit, and BC PNP candidates.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-11-15">November 15, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>6 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <figure class="mb-10">
      <img src="/images/articles/lmia-processing-times-november-2026.png" width="1200" height="630" alt="Four timer cards comparing October 2026 average LMIA processing times: 102 business days for high-wage, 94 for the permanent resident stream, 86 for low-wage and 9 for the Global Talent Stream."
           class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    </figure>

    <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 p-6 mb-10 rounded-r-xl">
      <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-2">Quick answer</p>
      <p class="text-red-900">
        On November 12, 2026, ESDC updated its <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/employment-social-development/services/foreign-workers/labour-market-impact-assessment-processing-times.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">LMIA processing times page</a> with October 2026 averages: 102 business days for high-wage LMIAs, 94 for the permanent resident stream, 86 for low-wage and 9 for the Global Talent Stream. A high-wage LMIA in B.C. (a job paying at least $38.40 an hour) now takes roughly four and a half to five months after the employer applies, before the worker even files a work permit application. BC PNP Skills Immigration does not require an LMIA.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">102</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Business days, high-wage stream (October 2026 average)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-slate-800 mb-2">94</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Business days, permanent resident stream</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-emerald-600 mb-2">9</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Business days, Global Talent Stream (10-day standard)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        A Labour Market Impact Assessment (LMIA) is the document most employers need from ESDC before they can hire a foreign worker on an employer-specific work permit. Because it comes before the work permit, every week of LMIA processing pushes back a worker's start date, and for people already in Canada it can decide whether a new permit arrives before the current one expires. This week's federal update shows the main streams used by B.C. employers taking three to five months on average. It is a quiet week otherwise: there has been no Express Entry round since October 28 and the BC PNP's latest draw was the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-draw-november-13-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">November 13 Care and Build round</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="numbers" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The November 12, 2026 LMIA Processing Times</h2>

      <p>
        ESDC's page, dated November 12, 2026, lists average processing times in business days for October 2026. The department says the figures can vary significantly from month to month, that the table is updated monthly, and that the advertising period an employer must complete before applying is not included.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 text-base">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">LMIA stream</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">Average, October 2026 (business days)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">Rough calendar time</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">High-wage stream</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">102</td><td class="px-4 py-2">About 20 working weeks</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Permanent resident stream</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">94</td><td class="px-4 py-2">About 19 working weeks</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Low-wage stream</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">86</td><td class="px-4 py-2">About 17 working weeks</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Agricultural stream</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">20</td><td class="px-4 py-2">About 4 working weeks</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Seasonal Agricultural Worker Program</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">11</td><td class="px-4 py-2">About 2 working weeks</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Global Talent Stream</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">9</td><td class="px-4 py-2">About 2 working weeks</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Source: ESDC, LMIA application processing times, page dated November 12, 2026. Calendar conversions are our approximation at five business days a week and ignore statutory holidays.</p>

      <p>
        CIC News, reporting the same update on November 12, compared it with ESDC's September 2026 figures and found increases of four business days in the high-wage, low-wage and seasonal agricultural streams, 15 days in the permanent resident stream (from 79 to 94), and a two-day drop in the agricultural stream. We could not check the September numbers against an archived copy of ESDC's page, so treat the month-over-month changes as reported rather than confirmed. The October figures above come directly from ESDC.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bc" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What It Means for B.C. Employers and Workers</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Most professional jobs fall in the slowest stream</h3>
      <p>
        ESDC decides whether a job is high-wage or low-wage by comparing the offered wage with the provincial threshold, which is the provincial median hourly wage plus 20%. For B.C., ESDC lists <strong>$38.40 an hour</strong> for applications received on or after July 17, 2026. Jobs at or above that wage, which covers many engineering, technology, health and management roles, go through the high-wage stream, now the slowest at 102 business days on average.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice, a B.C. employer who starts recruiting today must first complete the required advertising, then wait about 20 working weeks for a decision, and only then can the worker apply to IRCC for the permit. Our <a href="/articles/ircc-processing-times-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">IRCC processing times guide</a> covers that second wait.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Low-wage LMIAs are still closed in most large B.C. cities</h3>
      <p>
        The 86-day low-wage average only matters where ESDC will process the application. For low-wage applications submitted from October 9, 2026 to January 7, 2027, ESDC refuses to process them in census metropolitan areas with unemployment of 6% or more. Four B.C. areas are on that list: Kelowna (8.6%), Abbotsford-Mission (7.6%), Vancouver (7.0%) and Nanaimo (6.6%). Victoria (5.8%), Chilliwack (5.6%) and Kamloops (3.6%) are below the line. ESDC will refresh the table on January 8, 2027.
      </p>
      <p>
        Exempt sectors and occupations include primary agriculture, construction, food manufacturing, hospitals, and nursing and residential care facilities. On November 6, 2026, ESDC added early childhood educators and said low-wage LMIAs submitted from that date can cover up to two years of employment instead of one. Our November 12 roundup has <a href="/articles/express-entry-draws-paused-november-2026-immigration-roundup" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">more on those November 6 changes</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">PR-stream LMIAs have lost their Express Entry value</h3>
      <p>
        The permanent resident stream is for employers supporting a worker's permanent residence. Until this month, a positive LMIA was the usual way to make a job offer count for Express Entry points. That changed on November 3, 2026. IRCC's job offer page now says a job offer does not need an LMIA to qualify, and that having one will not add CRS points or give a profile priority. Job offer points now depend on whether the occupation is an eligible high-wage occupation, with IRCC still checking that the offer is genuine.
      </p>
      <p>
        So an employer waiting 94 business days for a PR-stream LMIA only to boost a worker's Express Entry score should check with a licensed representative whether the LMIA is still needed. IRCC also warns that its online profile system does not yet reflect the change and may still ask for LMIA details. It gives placeholder values to enter until the system is updated. For draw history and the pause, see our <a href="/articles/express-entry-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry draws tracker</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bcpnp" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">BC PNP: No LMIA Needed, but Status Still Matters</h2>
      <p>
        WelcomeBC says employers supporting a BC PNP applicant do not need a federal LMIA. What Skills Immigration requires is an eligible job offer from a B.C. employer, explained in our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-job-offer-requirements-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">job offer requirements guide</a>. That makes the BC PNP one of the few employer-driven routes in B.C. that skips the LMIA queue altogether.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catch is keeping legal status while you wait. Under its current Skills Immigration program guide, the BC PNP may issue a work permit support letter after nomination. The letter lets a nominee apply to IRCC for an employer-specific permit without an LMIA, but only on request and under conditions, for example a permit expiring soon and a PR application already submitted. A nomination on its own does not authorize work. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-work-permit-guide" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP work permit guide</a> covers the timing.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you hold an LMIA-based permit and hope to stay in B.C., the long LMIA queue is a reason to start your BC PNP registration early instead of planning to renew through a new LMIA. You can check where you stand with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> and compare with recent cutoffs on the <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP draws page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="next" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Dates to Watch</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>November 18, 2026:</strong> ESDC's foreign workers page says the fall 2026 Job Bank wage update takes place on this date. Job Bank wages are updated each fall, and employers should confirm that an offered wage still meets the figure for the occupation and region before they submit an LMIA.</li>
        <li><strong>Mid-December 2026:</strong> the next monthly processing-time update is expected, as ESDC updates the table monthly. No exact date is published.</li>
        <li><strong>January 8, 2027:</strong> ESDC's next refresh of the CMA unemployment table that decides where low-wage LMIAs are refused.</li>
        <li><strong>March 1, 2027:</strong> the date ESDC gave for proposed, province-opt-in changes to low-wage LMIA access. Details and participating provinces have not been published.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        This article is general information, not legal advice. Processing times are averages and individual files can be faster or slower.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
